{
  "dom_id": "step-q104069",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "$\\int 2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$",
  "worked_solution": "For this integral we choose $u=2{x}^{3}$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ Filling in the table gives the following:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same-color Products** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2{x}^{3}$ | $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ |  |  |\n| $6{x}^{2}$ | $-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}$ | $+$ | $-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ |\n| $12x$ | $-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}$ | $-$ | $\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}$ |\n| $12$ | $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{8}$ | $+$ | $\\frac{3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{16}$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}$ |\n\nWe add up all the terms of the last column to get the answer for our integral. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}+C \\\\  & =(3x-2{x}^{3})\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+(6{x}^{2}-3)\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
